description:
In decrypt.rs try_decrypt_kari(), the ephemeral originator public key is extracted as raw bytes from the OriginatorPublicKey BitString and passed directly to DecryptionKey::agree_ecdh():

  let ephemeral_bytes = match &kari.originator {
      OriginatorIdentifierOrKey::OriginatorKey(orig) => orig.public_key.raw_bytes().to_vec(),
      ...
  };
  let cek = key.agree_ecdh(&ephemeral_bytes, ukm, rek.enc_key.as_bytes(), &kari_alg)?;

The OriginatorPublicKey also contains an AlgorithmIdentifier (algorithm.parameters = curve OID), but the curve OID in the originator key is NOT cross-checked against the KDF scheme OID (kari.key_enc_alg.oid). A crafted KARI entry could supply a P-384 ephemeral key in a KARI declaring dhSinglePass-stdDH-sha256kdf-scheme (P-256 KDF scheme), and the mismatch is silently passed to agree_ecdh.

What goes wrong: if an implementor of agree_ecdh does not independently validate the curve of the ephemeral key, a small-subgroup or invalid-curve attack becomes possible — the attacker can cause the ECDH to produce a predictable shared secret Z, recovering the CEK with O(1) oracle queries (integrity break; potential full plaintext recovery). Even without a full attack, a curve mismatch produces a malformed DH output that may propagate as a wrong but structurally valid CEK.

Affected code: decrypt.rs lines 231-245 (try_decrypt_kari, ephemeral extraction and agree_ecdh call). The kari_alg derived from map_kari_alg carries the correct KDF scheme but is not used to validate the originator public key bytes.

Fix: before calling agree_ecdh, verify that orig.algorithm.oid == ID_EC_PUBLIC_KEY and that orig.algorithm.parameters (the curve OID) matches the curve implied by kari_alg.key_agreement (P-256 for StdDhSha256Kdf, P-384 for StdDhSha384Kdf). Return MalformedInput if they disagree.
